Delivered in #174 and squash-merged to `main` as `c1f71be66d278f0b9dc1adb3f6fe7b3280a5ed77`. The merged tree is identical to the reviewed head `ec33ab9`.

- **Push API:** qualified and recorded [above](https://github.com/jimmie-potts/agent-device-hub/issues/16#issuecomment-5789545093) before the design was settled.
- **Package:** `@jimmie-potts/tidbyt-controller` 0.1.0 under `controllers/tidbyt`. It is an in-process library with three parts:
  - a pure 64×32 lossless WebP renderer, whose golden images Pillow decodes independently;
  - a background-push cloud connection that reads credentials from a private mode-600 file;
  - one serialized queue on controller v1 tickets, revisions, generations and receipts, using the controller-local `tidbyt-display` profile.
- **Spec:** OpenSpec change `gh-16-tidbyt-controller`, archived; it adds the `tidbyt-cloud-controller` capability.
- **Review:** independent Standards and Specification reviews both approved `ec33ab9`. In round 1 both found the same P2: a result from a replaced connection could set holds again. It was fixed with a regression test, and the P3 items were addressed or documented.
- **CI:** all 6 Depot jobs passed on the PR head and again on merged `main` `c1f71be`.

The source uses fakes only. Nothing was installed, and no call reached the device or account beyond the one qualification push. Still open elsewhere:
- status integration: #19;
- installed, visible acceptance with explicit permission to replace the display: #21;
- Tronbyt: #23 and #24.

body:
## Outcome

A Codex approval marker that has no request ID clears once the owner retires its turn. The newer turn proves the approval was answered, so its task no longer shows blocked, and its wall Line stops showing red.

## Evidence (2026-09-24)

The installed owner at revision 1222 held 10 approval markers without request IDs, which kept 7 wall tasks blocked:
- 7 markers are on turns the owner already retired;
- 1 is on its session's current turn;
- 2 are on turns that were never selected.

The owner keeps attention across turns, and Codex's permission hook sends no request ID. So once a session moves on, nothing can ever resolve these markers, and a task stays red even while it is working on a later turn. Explicit recovery (#185) accepts only a marker on the current turn.

## Scope

- [ ] When the owner retires a turn, it removes the no-ID approval markers bound to that turn.
- [ ] A no-ID approval that arrives for an already retired turn is not retained.
- [ ] On startup, the owner removes stored no-ID approvals whose turns it has already retired, in one durable revision. The installed backlog therefore clears without waiting for new events.
- [ ] Unchanged:
  - markers on the current turn;
  - markers on turns the owner never selected, because ordering cannot prove those are older;
  - approvals with a request ID;
  - questions and input requests;
  - explicit recovery.
- [ ] No acknowledgment, permission decision or other state change is inferred.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Reducer tests cover each of these:
  - a no-ID approval clears when a new turn starts;
  - a current-turn marker stays;
  - an unselected-turn marker stays when another turn starts;
  - a late marker for a retired turn is not retained;
  - an approval with a request ID stays;
  - stored markers on retired turns clear at startup and stay cleared after a restart.
- [ ] Installed: after the update, tasks whose only markers are on retired turns stop showing blocked on the wall. One comment records the result.

## Dependencies

None. Nanoleaf already clears a frozen red when a later owner revision removes the approval on the same turn (codex-nanoleaf #72), and a new turn produces current evidence.

## Not included

Clearing markers on unselected or current turns without evidence, 24-hour expiry (#195), a dashboard recovery button, and changes to the Codex hook.

## Readiness

Complexity small: a reducer rule, startup normalization and tests. Uncertainty low. Impact medium: it changes attention shown to consumers. Delivery follows docs/sdlc.md.
